I added `ImportBatch` in `src/Core/Models/Data/ImportBatch.cs`, with a unit test. The 3 new tests pass under `dotnet test`. The build also printed existing CA1305/CA1304 locale warnings from other test files, which I did not touch.

```csharp
using Bit.Core.Utilities;

namespace Bit.Core.Models.Data;

public record ImportBatch(Guid Id, Guid UserId, DateTime CreationDate)
{
    public static ImportBatch Create(Guid userId)
        => new(CombGuid.Generate(), userId, DateTime.UtcNow);
}
```

Choices I made:

- **Reused the existing comb generator.** `CombGuid.Generate()` in `src/Libraries/Data/Utilities/CombGuid.cs` already writes the UTC timestamp into the last six bytes in SQL Server's byte order. I did not write a second one. `CoreHelpers.GenerateComb()` is marked obsolete and points to `CombGuid`, so I called `CombGuid` directly.
- **Positional record.** It keeps the type small and matches the three fields you listed.
- **Two `UtcNow` reads.** `Id` and `CreationDate` each read the clock, so they can differ by a few ticks. The timestamp-carrying overload that takes an explicit time is internal and marked test-only, so I did not use it.
- **Test file.** The tests are in `test/Core.Test/Models/Data/ImportBatchTests.cs`. They cover the user ID, the UTC creation date, a non-empty ID, and ordering of IDs created in sequence. The ordering test only checks the timestamp bytes and is weak. It does not prove index-friendly behaviour.

`dotnet` was not on the default PATH. I ran the tests with `~/.dotnet` added to PATH. I have not committed anything.
